\section{Research attempt}

\subsection{Literature, source context and scope}
The full original catalogue body and source qualifications are preserved above. The relevant primary workshop passages were read in the cached publisher report. Historical claims and subsequent attributed results are distinguished from the mathematical work below. A restricted example, standard lemma or known theorem is not counted as new progress on the entire original target. Missing assumptions and repeated or rhetorical extracts are not silently turned into different solved problems.

\subsection{Why target-order isometry is insufficient for identifiability}
The source establishes local quadratic convergence of a particular IRLS scheme under restricted-isometry assumptions, then asks about a better initialization radius and global convergence. We test the preliminary route of deriving global recovery from norm preservation on the target model alone. Let $\mathcal M_{r,s}$ consist of matrices of rank at most $r$ with at most $s$ nonzero rows. Assume $r,s\ge1$, with model orders capped by ambient dimensions when necessary.
\begin{proposition}
If a linear map $\mathcal A$ satisfies
\[
\|\mathcal A H\|_2^2\ge(1-\delta)\|H\|_F^2
\quad\hbox{for every }H\in\mathcal M_{2r,2s},\qquad0\le\delta<1,
\]
then two matrices $X,Y\in\mathcal M_{r,s}$ having residuals at most $\varepsilon$ against the same observations obey
\[
\|X-Y\|_F\le\frac{2\varepsilon}{\sqrt{1-\delta}}.
\]
In particular noiseless model-consistent recovery is unique. Norm preservation only on $\mathcal M_{r,s}$ does not imply uniqueness.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The difference $H=X-Y$ has rank at most $2r$ because its column space lies in the sum of the two column spaces, and its row support is contained in the union of two supports of size at most $s$. The residual triangle inequality gives $\|\mathcal AH\|\le2\varepsilon$. Combining with the assumed lower bound proves the estimate.
For failure at the undoubled order, regard vectors in $\mathbb R^2$ as $2\times1$ matrices and take $r=s=1$ and $\mathcal A(x)=x_1+x_2$. Every one-row-supported vector satisfies $|\mathcal Ax|^2=\|x\|^2$, so the restricted isometry constant on this model is zero. Nevertheless $e_1$ and $e_2$ both map to one. Their difference has two-row support and lies in the measurement kernel.
\end{proof}
For any reconstruction rule receiving that common observation, the triangle inequality shows that at least one of its errors on $e_1,e_2$ is at least $\|e_1-e_2\|/2=1/\sqrt2$. Randomizing the rule cannot avoid this lower bound on the larger expected error, by applying the same inequality to each output and averaging. This is an information obstruction, not a defect proved for the particular published IRLS recurrence.
Even doubled-order injectivity only controls matrices already inside the model. Intermediate IRLS iterates may have larger rank and row support, and the hypothesis gives no contraction estimate for their errors or for changing smoothing weights. Applying the displayed bound to all such iterates would be the first unjustified step in the proposed global-convergence argument.

\subsection{Verification and remaining gaps}
The two-column operator, one-sparse isometries and indistinguishable targets were checked exactly, and finite structured matrix differences were checked for the rank/support bounds and residual inequality. The primary paper's landing page and the workshop's explicit algorithm description were read; no new convergence theorem is attributed to them. A valid basin enlargement or global IRLS convergence result under stronger assumptions remains unproved.

